.TH BOOTSWAP 1M "1 November 2026" "amix-base"
.SH NAME
bootswap \- add the swap areas at boot
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /etc/init.d/swap start
.br
.B /etc/init.d/swap stop
.SH DESCRIPTION
The base package's swap step runs at every boot as
.B /etc/rc2.d/S90swap
and at shutdown as
.BR /etc/rc0.d/K05swap ,
in the place of the system's own swap script.
.PP
At boot it adds the swap lines of
.B /etc/vfstab
(file system type
.BR swap ),
and, as the system's script did,
.B /SWAPFILE
and
.B /usr/src/SWAPFILE
when they exist:
.TP
.I a file
is added whole.
.TP
.I a slice
.RB ( /dev/dsk/c\fIn\fPd\fIn\fPs\fIn\fP )
has its length read from the disk's partition table (with
.BR /usr/amiga/bin/rdb ).
The kernel may already swap on the start of the slice: when
.B "swap \-l"
lists no area on it, the step adds from block 0; when it lists one area
from block 0, the step adds from the next 16-block boundary past it; any
other layout is left alone.
How far the added area reaches is set by
.BR swap_add ,
below.
.PP
At shutdown the files are removed first, then every area after the first.
.PP
Each area added, and each one the kernel refused, is said on the console
and written to
.BR /var/adm/rc.log ,
with the
.B swap_add
size that was used.
.B "swap \-s"
reads 0 whatever is set up;
.B "swap \-l"
shows the areas.
.SH SETTINGS
In
.BR /etc/rc.conf (4):
.TP
.BI swap_add= "size ..."
the sizes to try for a slice, in order; default
.BR 30 .
A size is
.B all
(to the end of the slice) or a number of MiB counted from the start of the
slice, the kernel's own area included, so that
.B 30
means at most 61440 blocks of swap on the slice in all.
A size past the end of the slice is the end.
.IP
The first size that adds at least 2048 blocks (1 MB) is tried.
When the kernel refuses it for want of memory
.RB ( ENOMEM ),
the next size is tried; each size at most once, and a size that ends where
a tried one did is not tried again.
Any other failure ends the step for that slice.
.SH THE KERNEL'S LIMIT
For every area
.B "swap \-a"
adds, the kernel needs a page table (16 bytes for each 2 KiB page on the
68030) in one piece of a fixed 4 MiB kernel area.
On a 64 MB 68030 machine only about 48 MiB of swap fits there.
A kernel with the guard refuses a larger request with
.BR ENOMEM ,
which is what the list steps down on; a kernel without it waits forever on
such a request, and the boot with it.
So the list must suit the kernel:
.TP
.B 30
any kernel: 30 MiB in all is known to fit.
.TP
.B "all 48 30"
a kernel with the guard.
.PP
The installer writes the line for the kernel it installs.
.SH EXAMPLE
On a 68030 kernel with the guard, whose own area is the first 20476 blocks
of a 64 MiB slice,
.B "swap_add=all 48 30"
first asks for blocks 20480 to the end (110592 blocks); if the kernel
refuses, blocks 20480 to 98304 (77824 blocks); if it refuses again, blocks
20480 to 61440 (40960 blocks).
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/vfstab
the swap lines.
.TP
.B /etc/rc.conf
.BR swap_add .
.TP
.B /var/adm/rc.log
what was added and refused.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rc.conf (4),
.BR rc.subr (1M),
.BR swap (1M)
